     "rate": "+0%",
     "text": "On Halloween in two thousand eight, a mysterious nine page whitepaper landed on a cryptography mailing list, and while you might think a massive tech company created Bitcoin to speed up digital payments, an anonymous inventor named Satoshi Nakamoto actually built it to cut out banks entirely. On January third, two thousand nine, Satoshi launched the network by mining its genesis block, establishing a decentralized system with zero central authority running in the background. Then on May twenty-second, two thousand ten, a user traded ten thousand bitcoins for two Papa John's pizzas worth forty-one dollars. That famous dinner gave digital cash its very first real-world value."
    }
